WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.352
This example inserts another Influenza admission into the base table.

00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:07.367
Then it compares the live base-table count with the stored materialized-view count.

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:09.447
The base table changes immediately.

00:00:09.447 --> 00:00:12.782
The snapshot remains unchanged because it has not been refreshed.

00:00:12.782 --> 00:00:14.927
This demonstrates staleness directly.

00:00:14.927 --> 00:00:18.537
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:18.537 --> 00:00:23.832
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:23.832 --> 00:00:25.857
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:25.857 --> 00:00:30.802
In a real setting such as an analytics team exposing a stable reporting view to dashboard users,

00:00:30.802 --> 00:00:35.882
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:35.882 --> 00:00:37.667
before discussing syntax.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:42.880
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
